## Feature map

### UI localization (UIKey)

User-facing copy is wrapped in `IKey`, defined as static keys (typically in a `UIKeys` class), and resolved from the localization JSON. UI widgets take keys for labels and tooltips; raw English strings in panels are incorrect.

Detail: `docs/architecture/ui-localization.md`

### UI framework

The in-mod editor is a custom widget tree centered on `UIElement`, with common controls (buttons, toggles, colors, icons), flex-style `UI.row` / `UI.column` layouts, and `UIContext` for input and drawing. Full-screen work uses dashboard/panel bases; modals use overlay panels.

Detail: `docs/architecture/ui-framework.md`

### Values, registration, and model formats

Features register on common and client sides. Persistent form/replay/clip fields use the `Value` system for NBT/JSON and UI sync. Supported model asset formats include BBS, BOBJ, GEO, OBJ, and VOX, each with different rig/animation strengths.

Detail: `docs/architecture/values-and-registration.md`

### Forms

Forms define how actors and objects look. A form is data (`Form` + `Value` properties), a client renderer, and usually an editor panel. Body parts attach child forms to parent bones. Standard types include Model, Billboard, Label, Extruded, Block, Item, Mob, and Particle.

Detail: `docs/architecture/forms.md`

### ModelForm

`ModelForm` is the main animated-rig form: model id, base/overlay poses, action mappings, and shape keys. Rendering goes through an animator pipeline and a bone matrix cache used for attachments (body parts, items).

Detail: `docs/architecture/model-form.md`

### Particle form

Particle forms use a Bedrock-style subset, editable in-mod or via Snowstorm. Timeline tracks can pause emission and swap textures; MoLang user variables drive math-based effects from the film timeline.

Detail: `docs/architecture/particle-form.md`

### Replays

A replay stores motion keyframes and form property animation for an entity on the film timeline. Editing supports multi-replay bulk changes, onion-skin ghosts on stubs, and an optional Actor Mode flag that changes how the body exists in the world.

Detail: `docs/architecture/replays.md`

### Actor Mode

Actor Mode keeps two representations: a client stub (recording/onion/puppeteer source) and a physical world entity (`ActorEntity`, or the real player when `fp`). They must not be collapsed; toggling Actor never deletes the stub. This area covers spawn, draw vs skip, server/client sync, combat/death, relative camera rules, Iris shadows, and editor gizmos/UI toggle order.

Detail: `docs/architecture/actor-mode.md`

### Keyframes

Keyframes interpolate typed values over (sub-)ticks via channels, interpolation modes (including Bezier), and factories registered per type. Extending animatable data means implementing and registering an `IKeyframeFactory`. Related features include texture-sequence animation and anchor tracks between replays/bones.

Detail: `docs/architecture/keyframes.md`

### Clips

Clips are timeline segments for camera paths, effects, media, and utilities (remap, envelope, curves, dolly zoom, etc.). Camera clips cover path/keyframe/dolly/idle/look/orbit/tracker-style behaviors; action clips can run server and/or client logic on a frequency.

Detail: `docs/architecture/clips.md`

### Keybinds

BBS uses its own `KeyCombo` / `Keybind` / `KeybindManager` stack, separate from vanilla `KeyMapping`. Combos are declared (e.g. in `Keys.java`) and bound to actions in UI contexts; some important editor shortcuts are global rather than panel-local.

Detail: `docs/architecture/keybinds.md`

